<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hatuey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1512</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hatuey was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> chief from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> who fled to eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and led resistance against the Spanish conquest under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</a>. Captured, he was burned alive in 1512. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> recorded that he refused baptism, saying he did not wish to go to a heaven where Spaniards were; he is honoured in Cuba as its first national hero.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hatuey.jpg" alt="Hatuey" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michal Zalewski · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hatuey_monument,_Baracoa,_Cuba.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
